Date: Tue, 9 Feb 93 17:39:27 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Brave new smokeless world

>"But if the studies are true, isn't exposing a child to secondary smoke
>a form of assault and battery?"  --Dale Worley
>
>Sure, but the question is, who brings the suit?  
>-- Marc Ringuette

I think Marc gives both the wrong answer and the wrong restatement of the
question.

Exposing a child to secondary smoke could almost never be a form of
assault.  Assault is an act intended to put a reasonable person in imminent
fear of offensive contact.  Smokers almost never deliberately threaten to
blow smoke on kids.

Exposing a child to secondary smoke could only be a form of battery in
certain circumstances.  In many cases of exposure to 2nd hand smoke, the
child (or the child's legal guardian) will have assumed the risk of
contact.  Thus, for example, if the child voluntarily enters a restaurant
(or the child's legal guardian causes the child to enter a restaurant)
where people are known to smoke, that child has no cause of action for
battery.  The kid will have "walked into the punch."  A suit for battery
will be available only if the child is harmed by second hand smoke where he
or she has an independent right to be free of such smoke.  The presumption,
of course, is that the child has such a right; but this presumption is
relatively easy to overcome because smoking is so often done on private
property among mutually consenting parties.

Marc also errs, I think, in focusing on the question of who brings suit. 
As my analyses of the assault and battery claims reveal, the issue of
whether or not there is a claim has little to do with who sues.  That
question is important in other contexts, but not this one.

The more important question, I think, is who is being sued.  Is it the
person smoking, the parent who allows the kid to be exposed to smoke, or
both?

But even this question pales in importance once we see that there are only
very slim grounds to sue smokers for battery, and even slimmer ones for
suing them for assault.


