Date: 9 Feb 93 23:34:43 GMT
From: ssandfort@attmail.com
Subject: META: Burden of proof reform

sssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssss
Robin Hanson wrote:

    "I dislike the phrase BURDEN OF PROOF, as it conotes
    more certainty and persuasive power than can reasonable
    be expected from arguments on the subjects we discuss.
    Rather let's say that we have a PRESUMPTION AGAINST
    positions against our current shared positions."

The phrase, "Burden of Proof" (BoP) is words of art in law,
debate and (I think) logic.  As far as I know, it has nothing to
do with mathematics, nor does it mean "beyond a reasonable
doubt."  It means simply that the proponent of a position must
make a "prima facie case" in support of that position.  This is
how I used it when I originally posted "The Great Debate."  I
believe that is also what Tom Morrow had in mind in his "Reform"
post.  Actually, Robin's "PRESUMPTION AGAINST" sentence, is a
fairly good definition of BoP.  BoP has a well established
meaning and a long history of usage.  I think it is the correct
term for our purposes.


 S a n d y  S a n d f o r t                ssandfort@attmail.com


P.S.  I've noticed that several posts have used the expression,
"beyond a reasonable doubt," to describe the BoP in law suits.
This is incorrect.  "Reasonable doubt" applies only to *criminal*
cases.  The BoP in civil cases is usually expressed as "by the
preponderance of the evidence."  In crude terms, criminal cases
require 99% proof, while civil cases require only 51% proof.
