Date: Tue, 9 Feb 93 19:20:59 CST
From: lists@alan.b30.ingr.com (Alan Barksdale (lists))
Subject: MEME: Voluntary Social Engineering Through Memetics

[...]
> I believe it was the Grand Inquisitor in Dostoyevsky's "The Brother's
> Karamazov" who argued that people *do not want* freedom (and therefore
> would prefer a religion to tell them what to do with their lives).
[...]
>                               Kevin Q. Brown

I long have thought that a good way to promote freedom indirectly is through
engineered religion.  I have read claims that certain aspects of Christianity
(e.g., history as progress toward goals rather than as a cycle) and especially
Protestantism (e.g., the Will of God knowable to any man who reads the Bible
carefully rather than from the Church hierarchy, an attititude that the
possessor may be tempted to apply in other areas), had much to do with "the
rise of the West".  If memetics could be a science as well as an entertaining
simile--an assertion of which I am not convinced--might not memetic
engineering create a religious meme complex with pro-freedom side effects?  By
analogy with genetic engineering, one would not try to create a meme complex
from scratch but instead would modify a successful existing one.  I would
suggest starting with some sect of Christianity or of Islam.

One would have to discover what causes a religion to grow and what religious
doctrines have desirable effects, probably extremely difficult tasks,
especially since the environment in which meme complexes live changes over
time.  My amateur investigations into fringe religious groups has made me
bellyfeel the immense number and variety of such cults in the past as well as
the present:  Millerites, Breatharians, Rosicrucians, Creativists, etc.
Mutations develop continually; only a few survive, and fewer still prosper.  I
believe that the successes come from those few cult founders who develop meme
complexes that meet the religious needs of a significant number of hosts in an
environment where those needs are not currently satisfied.  I think such a
niches result from changes to the underlying human society, e.g.,
technological and economic developments that make it profitable to free
serfs.  I hypothesize that the developed countries have undergone so much
change in the last several generations that they are ripe for new religions or
religion substitutes, of which Marxism is a beleagured example.  The side
effects of a religious meme complex are as hard to predict as is its lifespan,
though careful study of history should help.

However, the enterprise I suggest would have the advantage that its creators
would take an engineering attitude.  I think that chance has much to do with
the success or failure of a religion, for the founders of naturally occurring
religions often are not willing or able to change them systematically to fit
their environment.  Hence it is those religions that just happen to fill an
open niche that succeed.  I believe a religious meme complex would have a
better chance if its infection mechanisms were developed using the best
available advertising and psychological knowledge by people guided by what
works rather than by strongly-held religious beliefs.  Furthermore, the
temptation to have an entirely satifactory theory of what one is doing before
one goes further would have to be resisted; it is better to succeed partly
with empiricism than to research forever.

The prospect of testing the side effects of mutated religions frightens me,
for doing so would be difficult except in the world, and one might create a
fast-growing religion with undesirable side effects, e.g., pro-censorship
memes.  It would be safer to start by trying to produce desirable effects on
the believers of unsuccessful or geographically-limited cults before applying
the lessons learned to religions which one wants to modify for growth.

One problem would be persuading people to accept engineered religious meme
complexes, for I doubt the enterprise could be kept secret.  One could use an
argument similar to one I heard in Sunday School as a child, "God caused the
Roman Empire to exist so that reliable transportion and communication would be
possible over a large part of the ancient world, which situation allowed
Christianity to spread rapidly; God uses non-Christian organizations for his
own purposes; He works in mysterious ways."  Unfortunately, I expect many
people would still feel an esthetic distaste for an engineered religion.
However, one advantage to starting with an existing religion is that some of
its followers might be persuaded to join the effort if they were convinced
that a memetic engineering approach would help them spread their religion
more widely and that the product would be reasonably orthodox.  Furthermore,
if the project were successful, I expect some number of the nonbelieving
participants would convert.  The participation of both believers and
nonbelievers with different but noncontradictory motives would lend strength
to the God-works-in-mysterious-ways argument.

If this project yielded generally useful methods, one might reach other hosts
not susceptible to the initial meme complex by modifying other religions,
e.g., Islam if one started with Christianity, or religion substitutes, e.g.,
stoicism.

I believe that overlaps in ethical codes among various cultures exist because
the similar parts reflect cultural or genetic adaptations to analogous
conditions.  One might save effort in the creation of more than one engineered
meme complex by creating modular methods to promote the shared ethical values.
These common ethics probably would not form a complete ethical system, which
instead would be provided by them added to any of the overlapping religions or
religion substitutes.  An effort in this direction is the appendix to C.S.
Lewis's _Mere_Christianity_ in which Lewis defines what he calls "natural
law", i.e., common ethics, by listing analogous passages in the sacred
writings of several religions.  (Lewis's version of Christianity in that book
might make a good starting point for an engineered religion.)

Such are my Dawkinsian daydreams.
