> Sure, but the question is, who brings the suit?  In totally civil PPL, like
> the ones a lot of us seek, it's up to the victim to bring suit or sell their
> right to do so, and if they choose not to, that's their business.
> 
> So what do we do about children?  It occurs to me that I don't have a canned
> answer.  Empowering a state to bring suit on their behalf would be an
> unfortunate move towards intrusive statism.  But assuming that parents have
> their childrens' best interests at heart does allow some of the worst
> atrocities in our society to occur unchecked.
> 
> The best I can come up with off the top of my head:  support PPLs which
> allow _anyone_ to bring suit on a child's behalf, with the proviso that they
> not personally profit from the suit.  If the ruling goes against the parents,
> the court can assign custody of the child to the best (for the child)
> volunteer who came forward at the time of the suit.  Of course, it will be
> important to be very liberal in deciding when a child can decide for
> himself/herself whether to bring suit and whether to stay with the parents.  

This system sounds like a very bad idea. Among other things, it would
allow busybodies who didn't like the way you were raising your children
to make a pain of your life.

Overall, the way you handle children in a PPL system depends very much on
the model of children you use in your PPL system. Are children property, as
in Roman law? Are they wards? Are they independant entities, and if so,
from what age? Its all a matter of the model. I won't say that I necessarily
have an answer to this question, but I'll give a few observations.

First, and most importantly, its rare for parents and children to have to come
into a legal conflict situation in the first place. Any mechanism that
arises should take into account that parents generally love their children
and that most of the time their legal relationship can remain entirely
informal.

Second, any such system should avoid making it easy for people to annoy
peaceful citizens who simply have different notions of child rearing. Whats
one man's neglect is another mans permitting a child to have needed
independance. Overall, I don't think its the place of society to intervene
in such decisions.

Third, any such system should make it possible for children who've achieved
sufficient personal independance to leave home at will regardless of parental
wishes once the child has gained sufficient reasoning ability to make an
informed decision. Its unfair to force a sufficiently precocious 14 year old
to remain with their parents. At the same time, however, such a system should
not make it easy for an abusive stranger to somehow bribe a small child
into making such a decision.

Anyway, having presented this quandry, I go, noting that I have no real
solution to this problem myself, but also that I think that the statists
have no solution either.

Perry

