Subject: META/PPL capricious?

Tim May voiced some thoughts about Singh v. Metzger, the purposes of this
list's PPL, and the possibility that PPL may be inherently capricious.
(This summary does not do Tim justice [should I expect to be sued?] but I
don't want to repeat his longish post.)

A precedent-setting decision -- that is, a decision on a question where
custom provides no advice -- is necessarily "capricious".  A. P. Herbert put
it entertainingly in "Misleading Cases:  Dahlia, Ltd. v. Yvonne", in which
the barrister for the respondent pointed out that a decision of the House of
Lords (the court of last resort in Britain) fits 
	"the definition of an Act of God laid down by Lord Mildew in
	Turbot v. The Mayor of Swindon -- `something which no reasonable
	man could have expected'."
He goes on to say:
	"Milord, it must be evident a priori that no reasonable man 
	can foresee a decision of the House of Lords, for otherwise
	no reasonable man would appeal to the House of Lords, only
	to lose his case."

Tim also says:
> (A sidenote: If _restitution_ is the goal of PPL, and not
> _punishment_, then why is our list set up the way it is? Might it not
> be useful to look for ways of simulating restitution? Just a thought.)

Tim may be reading "restitution" too narrowly.  It does not consist only (or
at all, necessarily) in paying the plaintiff, but in restoring the happy
state before the offense.  In this case, punishing a flamer by banishment
from the list has the beneficial effect of restoring flame-free peace (of a
kind) to the list as a whole.

