It may be putting the cart before the horse to bemoan the tendency of
educated people to have fewer children.

I saw an article once (sorry I can't remember where -- it was at least
fourteen years ago) that said measured intelligence is negatively correlated
with birth order and family size.  The parents have a limited supply of time
to teach the children, and as each new child is added, that time is sliced
finer.

(There's an exception to the pattern:  the firstborn of two is likely to be
brighter than an only child.  Evidently I learned something from having a
kid sister.)

So maybe the bright people have fewer children partly because we inherit (by
genes or otherwise) a predisposition to small families.

(I have been called clever and I can't think of anyone in my family, since
my great-grandparents, who had more than three children.)

