I'm with Duncan Frissell on nation-states arising ca. 1600.  The ancient
world had mostly city-states, not nation-states.  Athens and Sparta were
city-states.  After Alexander the Great of Macedon conquered all the
city-states in Greece and much of the known world, too, the resulting
empire wasn't a city-state any longer, but I'm at a loss to name it.

Similarly, Rome began as a city-state, but soon expanded into the second
multicultural empire in Western history (Alexander's being the first).
By the way, this was when the radical jurisprudential innovation of
natural law originated, as the prohibitions common to all cultures of
the Roman empire were codified and enforced.

China may qualify as a nation-state, but, since "nation" means race,
the Chinese would have to be a race.  Classifying Asians as a race would
be consistent with classification of Negroids, Mongoloids, Caucasoids,
etc., but Chinese?  This may disqualify China from nation-statehood.

I'm neither so sure about the demise of the nation-state or, if it does
fall, that some new form of state will arise to fill the vacuum.  I posted
a while ago about how I see the Therapeutic State rising to fill the shoes
of the Redistributionist State, if you remember.  Socialism is dead; Fascism,
a.k.a. National Socialism, seems all too alive and well.

While I'm on the subject of history, Alexander Chislenko, if memory serves,
claimed that the USA was on its way to Empire.  I disagree.  The rise of
the American Empire occurred a long, long time ago.  I think it's on the
fall, not the rise.  We're living in the decline of the American Empire.

I'm partial to Jeff Hummel's interpretation of American history (for those
who may not know, he's an economics professor at Golden Gate University,
and a radical anarco-capitalist politically and a follower of the Austrian
