theorists, contemporary conservatives are Hobbesian, Redistributionists
and Therapeuticists are Rousseauean, and we libertarians are Lockean.  But
I digress.

These four great libertarian movements all advanced the cause of liberty,
but were halted by the Civil War.  The Civil War saw the triumph of
the northern-nationalist neo-mercantilist Whig agenda: high tariffs,
Federal banking regulation, etc.

A talk Jeff gave leads me to believe that this is when the American Empire
was founded.  It wasn't very expansionist for quite a while, but that great
American liberal William Graham Sumner wrote how Spain conquered the USA
when the USA conquered the Spanish Empire's possessions of Cuba and the
Phillipines.  Sumner thought that the point at which American Imperialism
rose - the 1890s.

However, if you consider the conquest of the South by the North as an
outgrowth of the Whig agenda to be expansionist, and take into account
other parts of Lincoln's prosecution of the war, then I think the
Imperialism becomes quite clear.

Lincoln wrote in the 1830s that there was a danger that an American
Napolean Bonaparte would rise that would stop at nothing, whether
emancipating slaves or enslaving free men, it his pursuit of power.
Ironically, Lincoln seems to have fulfilled his own prophecy.

But enough history.  Smoking!  The whole issue of second-hand smoke and
