First a comment about Krzys' and his postings, and then a comment on Dale's
original point about overbuilding.

Krzys' writes, quoting Dale Worley:

>The point is
>that people who are *trying* to make a profit will (toward the end of
>a development boom) build a significant number of projects that are
>"unneeded" in that the space they contain is unsalable.
>says Dale
>
>This point is totally insignificant as such behavior will not change
>in any way the total amount of funds available for profits in the economy.
>And profits are the engine of the civilization, so why bother attacking
>profits, why not just be honest and say that we should go back to
>nature, kill off excessive number of people, eliminate all products
>of civilization, eat raw meat and whatever grows on trees, or maybe even
>better -- why not just have all people killed and let nature take
>over.

Please, Dale's comment didn't warrant such a flame!  Krzys' seems to be
implying Dale is in favor of the list of things he beats him over the head
with!  Dale seems to be at least as liberty-loving as most on this list
and, in fact, has been one of the better contributors these last several
weeks. For a "newbie," he's certainly above the average!

Krzys' has made some good arguments, at times, but this frequent method of
arguing by "escalating" the rhetoric (some would say by raising "straw
men") is getting a little old. None of us in favor of going back to nature,
killing off excessive people, eating raw meat, or letting nature take over.
Rebutting a position one doesn't agree with by listing all sorts of
extremes that have little or nothing to do with the position is a lousy
debating style.

To perhaps make the point harshly, imagine how Krzys' would feel if he were
to be attacked by anti-Randite rhetoric, such as: "Why not make us all wear
flowing black capes and smoke cigarettes, the way Rand did? After all, A is
A and you Randroids all want us to pay for your Rand-cherished space
program, right?" Not very persuasive, is it?

(Note to Harry Shapiro and other List Officials: Despite rereading the
latest list posting rules, I am unclear if merely _commenting_ on the
posting style of another List member is some kind of violation of the
rules. Things have gotten pretty confusing these last several weeks.
However, since I've seen no action taken against the various posters who a.
fail to use prefixes, b. say they're "really pissed" or "have had it," and
c. call other postings "specious" and the like, I am assuming these
relatively mild comments are not in fact a violation of the latest List
rules. If they are, I stand ready to take my punishment like a man--I mean,
like an Extropian. And on the bright side, a one day suspension of posting
privileges might let me get more work done! :-} )

On the subject of Dale's original comment, it seems likely that
"overbuilding" will almost always occur in a free market, as various agents
judge and misjudge future demands. To have precisely the "needed" amount of
housing or rental space built would be unlikely (and perhaps
unworkable....). Sometimes rental markets are tight (less than a few
percent vacancies), sometimes they're not (lots of vacancies). The loss to
the builder (in lower rents or sales prices) is the gain to the renter or
buyer.

In a later posting, Perry writes: "I'm not going to let you get away with
this one, either. I want to hear references that say that a significant
percentage of projects are unprofitable." 

I don't know if Dale was claiming that the _net_ profits of real estate
developers over the long term are negative (a situation that can't last
long, one would think), or if he was noting that speculative excesses can
and do occur (frequently, too).

I'm not a real estate statistician, so I can't easily put my finger on the
kind of proof Perry wants Dale to provide, but there seems to be ample
evidence that "overbuilding" (in the sense mentioned above, of vacancies
above the regional norm, of cash flow problems for the
investors/developers, etc.) does in fact occur. Sometimes projects are
begun and conditions change, whether they be the recessions that Perry
mentions or just regional changes. For a variety of reasons, buildings,
malls, houses, etc., may get built and become a glut on the market. (This
doesn't mean the deals are unprofitable, of course, nor does it mean the
developer ultimately loses money...he presumably has other ventures which
more than compensate for the losing deals.)

Markets in action. I wouldn't want it any other way. 

Of course, distortions occur, as when the government subsidizes building,
as tax write-offs, as amelioration of perceived problems, whatever.

Have you heard about "neutron buildings"? The investors are destroyed, but
the buildings remain standing. (A situation that has occurred many times,
recently in Houston and other Sunbelt boomtowns. In that case, the
taxpayers "bailed out" many of the investors...and Houston was left with a
glut of fairly cheap rental space.)

-Tim

